## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well: the
frame loop and the elapsed time it measures, fitting the fixed logical stage onto
the canvas, the keyboard input the player drives the critter and the menus with,
image loading, the audio, and the on-screen debug overlay. The specification in
`specs/` states what Floe needs from that layer and fixes nothing else about it.

The simulation runs on a fixed step, and the accumulator that turns a frame's
elapsed time into whole ticks is part of what you write. `specs/overview.md`
states the rule.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the game
and does not restate the engine. The documentation also carries a complete worked
example of unit-testing a game built on it.

Build the game on the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame
loop and the delta time it hands your update, the canvas fit, the input actions,
the audio, asset loading, and the on-screen debug overlay. It also holds the
game's state, by value: each frame it hands `update` the current state read-only
and keeps what `update` returns as the next, so a frame builds the next state
from the current one rather than writing into it. Register what the game needs
with the engine and use what it gives back. What remains yours is the game
itself: the strait and its sixteen lanes, the critter's hop, the bear that hunts
it, the bays, the run, and everything it draws.
{{else}}
